  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Addressing disaster risk through sustainable environmental management in Sierra Leone</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">By focusing on sustainable land use management and agricultural activities, this project directly supports the MDG1, &#x201c;to eradicate extreme poverty and hunger&#x201d; and MGD7 &#x201c;to ensure environmental sustainability&#x201d;. This project aims to reduce the vulnerability of target communities to drought, bush fires and wind storms through sustainable environmental management. 10 communities in Kenema and Makeni diocesan areas in Sierra Leone are regularly affected by these hazards, the affects of which are in turn exacerbated by poor environmental management practices. The combination of poor environmental management practices and regular hazards results in hunger, poverty and reduced food security for the target communities in both the short and long term.  The project will improve the target community&#x2019;s understanding and implementation of sustainable environmental management and agricultural practices that will reduce the target community&#x2019;s vulnerability to hazards, improve their food security, reduce hunger and poverty, and ensure long term environmental sustainability.  
This proposed project will support 10 target communities to implement sustainable agricultural activities, including inter-cropping, fencing, and the use of drought resistant crops. The project will enable these communities to take practical steps such as planting trees to create windbreaks and stabilise the soil, and provide them with eco-stoves and water storage facilities. Finally, the project will support the 10 target communities to develop community disaster risk reduction action plans, enabling the communities to evaluate the hazards and the links between these hazards and their existing land use management practices, thereby enabling them to respond with small scale practical activities that reduce their vulnerability to both the hazard, and the resulting food insecurity.</narrative>
  </description>
